A||||11||Alex4mula||Help me on Brake pads selection please..||||1||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||10-26-2000||12:16 PM||||Check yesterday & one of my rear calipers is stucking and will need new pads. Will buy new rear calipers, all pads and also braided lines.<BR>For normal street driving, which pads will stop better? Stock? Carbomet? other semimetalic? Sometime ago I read some bad comments about Fiero Store carbomet. Experiences please? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||10-26-2000||12:33 PM||||Alex,<P>Look up grassroots motorsports. They have an excellent article on not only pad selection, but it also goes into some detail about why some pads work better in some situations.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000002||Alex4mula||10-26-2000||03:57 PM||||Thanks WT. Very good article. Still I would like to hear from your experiences here. Any pad brand that I should avoid?||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||10-26-2000||03:58 PM||||I've heard to avoid the carbon-metallics (?). I say, get the cheapest, softest pads. You'll replace em sooner, but you'll be able to stop.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||10-26-2000||05:27 PM||||Mmm? That's what I heard also but why exactly? Does semi-metalic & carbon-metalic supose to be better even for street use? I don't plan to autocross. So if stock ones will stop better in the street that's what I want. I think my current ones are semi or carbon-met & I don't feel the stop good enough. But I don't remember the performance of my stock ones...||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||10-26-2000||05:39 PM||||Friends,<P>If your only use is street use many folks (myself included) reccomend the old type of organic pad.  No they will not stop you as fast as the newer harder compounds, nor will they last as long.  Heck they even emit more brake dust, but my daily driver is just that practical.  Organic pads are cheaper and softer.  That translates into easier on your rotors.  I would rather replace 10 sets of pads than have to purchase new rotors... just more work and $ really.  <P>If you are driving for performance it is a different matter all together.  You will need to replace your rotors often and that extra grip could mean the difference of "getting punted off in church" (Reference for the BOC Gasses Tour fans), most racers will choose the extra grip.  <P>Temperatures are a major differece as well.  Organic pads get hot quickly and hold heat a long time.  This translates into more fade... but for street use, how often are you required to haul the car down from 60 MPH within a short period?  <P>Anyway... this is just my take on the situation.<P>William T. Thorn, III <P>||||reg||3
Z||000006||robertjay||10-26-2000||06:59 PM||||I agree with Wil, the stock pads work great for normal everyday driving.  The semi-metallic pads will wear out your rotors faster than the pads will wear out.  When I bought my 85 GT, it had semi-metallic pads, and the brakes plain sucked, but they were not worn out!  The rotors were actually worn out and the pads still had about 3/16" til the rivets.  Installed new rotors and GM pads, and they work much better now.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick's Dad||10-26-2000||07:19 PM||||As I clicked "reply," I was thinking that I had the opposite experience, but now I think that my perspective is different.<P>I used the carbomets, and found braking to be better. Can I quantify this? No, not without deferring to one of my fellow board member's "a$$ometer." What I observed was a clean, linear stop that got me out of damage when there was no right or left to go.<P>Much of my driving was within the route 128 (Interstate 93/95) area in Boston, though now I venture a little further south. I have never seen drivers who are as just plain awful as here (I have driven in NYC, DC, LA, and other places). The pads certainly helped.<P>That said, yes, I replaced my rotors practically at the same rate as the pads.<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||10-26-2000||09:30 PM||||Thank you all for sharing. I'll check the price of GMs. If they are cheaper than FS carbomet I may consider them. If not then will try Carbomet but will check the temp range in those. My main use is street but at some time may try some local autox here. Guess they may help then.||||reg||1
Z||000009||MrPBody||10-26-2000||09:37 PM||||Specifically, does anyone have good or bad experience with Fiero Store's carbomet pads?  Based on the Grassroots Motorsports article, I'm leaning toward a street carbon-metallic pad, as I nearly crammed my SE into the rear of a van a few days ago.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000010||Rodney||10-26-2000||10:57 PM||||I have heard of a Brute Force pad? Not sure who makes them but I heard they supply 15% more braking ability. If I was to install new brake pads I would look for those. Anyone know who makes these? I thought I had a email saved on them but I can't find it. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Wannabe||10-26-2000||11:08 PM||||Check out KVR performance. Very nice pads all around for street and hard use, but very reasonable priced.||||reg||1
